---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const controlledSnippet = `const [tab, setTab] = useState("account");

<Tabs value={tab} onValueChange={setTab}>…</Tabs>   // controlled
<Tabs defaultValue="account">…</Tabs>                // uncontrolled`;

const menuSnippet = `import { DropdownMenu, MenuItem } from "@kernelui-lib/react";

<DropdownMenu
  render={<Button>Edit</Button>}
  onOpenChange={(open, details) => {
    // Keep the menu open while someone works through it with the keyboard.
    if (!open && details.reason === "item-keyboard") details.cancel();
  }}
>
  <MenuItem onSelect={bold}>Bold</MenuItem>
  <MenuItem onSelect={italic}>Italic</MenuItem>
</DropdownMenu>`;

const analyticsSnippet = `<Select
  label="Plan"
  onValueChange={(plan, { reason }) => {
    // reason: "item-press" | "keyboard" | "typeahead"
    track("plan_changed", { plan, via: reason });
  }}
>
  …
</Select>`;

const dialogSnippet = `<Dialog
  open={open}
  title="Edit profile"
  onOpenChange={(next, details) => {
    // A stray click on the backdrop shouldn't throw away unsaved edits.
    if (dirty && details.reason === "outside-press") {
      details.cancel();
      return;
    }
    setOpen(next);
  }}
>
  …
</Dialog>`;

const elementsSnippet = `<kernel-tabs default-value="account">…</kernel-tabs>
<script type="module">
  document.querySelector("kernel-tabs").addEventListener("valuechange", (event) => {
    const { value, reason } = event.detail;   // reason: "trigger-press" | "keyboard" | …
    if (value === "billing" && !confirmLeave()) event.preventDefault();
  });
</script>`;

const fuzzySnippet = `import { fuzzyFilter, fuzzyScore } from "@kernelui-lib/react";

fuzzyScore("nwfl", "New file");
// → { score: 16.5, ranges: [[0, 1], [2, 3], [4, 5], [6, 7]] }

fuzzyFilter(commands, query, (command) => [command.label, ...command.keywords]);
// → matches only, best first, each with { item, score, ranges, field }`;
---

<BaseLayout title="State & changes" description="Controlled and uncontrolled state in Kernel, the reason behind every change, and how to cancel one." withSidebar>
  <DocsSidebar slot="sidebar" />
  <div class="docs-prose">
    <h1>State &amp; changes</h1>
    <p>
      Every stateful component can be controlled or uncontrolled. Pass
      <code>value</code> (or <code>checked</code>, <code>pressed</code>,
      <code>open</code>) to own the state yourself, or a
      <code>defaultValue</code> and let the component keep it. Either way the
      matching callback — <code>onValueChange</code>,
      <code>onCheckedChange</code>, <code>onOpenChange</code> — tells you about
      each change. <code>onChange</code> stays the native DOM event.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={controlledSnippet} lang="tsx" />

    <h2>Why it changed</h2>
    <p>
      Each callback gets a second argument, <code>details</code>:
      <code>{"{ reason, event, cancel(), isCanceled }"}</code>. The
      <code>reason</code> is a string union specific to the component —
      Tabs reports <code>"trigger-press"</code> or <code>"keyboard"</code>,
      Dialog reports <code>"close-button"</code>, <code>"escape-key"</code>,
      <code>"outside-press"</code> or <code>"programmatic"</code> — so a
      handler can treat a click differently from a key press, or keep
      programmatic changes out of analytics. <code>event</code> is the DOM
      event behind the change, when there is one. Each component's reasons
      are listed in its API table, typed as <code>ChangeDetails&lt;Reason&gt;</code>.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={analyticsSnippet} lang="tsx" />
    <p>
      It's purely additive: a callback that takes one argument never sees it.
    </p>

    <h2>Cancelling a change</h2>
    <p>
      The component calls your callback <em>before</em> it updates anything.
      Call <code>details.cancel()</code> and it keeps its current state and
      skips side effects such as closing a menu.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={menuSnippet} lang="tsx" />
    <p>
      A controlled component is already yours to keep: not updating your state
      leaves the old value in place, and <code>cancel()</code> just says so —
      check <code>details.isCanceled</code> if one handler both cancels and
      sets state.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={dialogSnippet} lang="tsx" />
    <p>
      Where the platform owns the change, cancelling does the closest honest
      thing. A native <code>popover</code>'s light-dismiss can't be refused,
      so a cancelled Popover or DropdownMenu close re-shows the panel before
      the next paint, without replaying its entrance. A cancelled Sheet
      drag-dismiss springs back to where it rested. A TagInput removal is
      reported once the chip's exit transition has played, so cancelling it
      brings the chip back; a paste or typed separator that adds several tags
      is one change, in both packages.
    </p>

    <h2>Web Components</h2>
    <p>
      The custom elements carry the same reasons on their existing events'
      <code>detail.reason</code>. Those events are now dispatched before the
      change commits and are cancelable: <code>event.preventDefault()</code>
      vetoes the change. Event names are unchanged; elements whose only
      change event was the native, uncancelable <code>change</code>
      (Checkbox, RadioGroup, Select, NumberField) add a cancelable
      <code>valuechange</code> that fires first. Dialog and CommandPalette
      ask with a cancelable <code>cancel</code> (the native dialog's own name
      for "about to close"); Popover, DropdownMenu and Accordion items with
      <code>beforetoggle</code>.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={elementsSnippet} lang="html" />

    <h2>Filtering</h2>
    <p>
      Combobox and CommandPalette filter with a ranked fuzzy match: the
      characters you type have to appear in order, not together, and results
      that match at the start, at word starts, or in one unbroken run rise to
      the top. Case and accents are ignored, and the matched characters are
      underlined. Both take a <code>filter</code> prop to replace it, and the
      matcher is exported for your own lists.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={fuzzySnippet} lang="tsx" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
